{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"The user is asking for a small Python CLI script notes.py:\n- `add TEXT` — appends a line with a date to notes.txt\n- `show` — displays the notes\n- Uses only the standard library\n- Keep it short\n- Run both once (test)\n\nLet me write the script.\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import date\n\nNOTES = \"notes.txt\"\n\ndef main():\n    args = sys.argv[1:]\n    if not args:\n        sys.exit(\"usage: notes.py add TEXT | notes.py show\")\n    if args[0] == \"add\":\n        if len(args) < 2:\n            sys.exit(\"missing TEXT\")\n        with open(NOTES, \"a\") as f:\n            f.write(f\"{date.today()} {\\\" \\\".join(args[1:])}\\n\")\n    elif args[0] == \"show\":\n        with open(NOTES) as f:\n            sys.stdout.write(f.read())\n    else:\n        sys.exit(f\"unknown command: {args[0]}\")\n\nmain()\n```\n\nWait, using sys.exit inside main before the main() call — let me restructure: define main and call it. Actually, `sys.exit` works fine anywhere.\n\nKeep it short. Then run `python3 notes.py add \"first note\"` and `python3 notes.py show`.\n\nLet me check the working directory first, or just write it. Write the file, then run both commands. Running add and show — show depends on add, so add first, then show. Two sequential bash calls are needed.\n","seq":98,"t":34008,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":2560,"draft_n":861,"draft_n_accepted":410,"predicted_ms":4290.0,"predicted_n":533,"prompt_ms":150.0,"prompt_n":98},"to_request":3,"usage":{"cached_tokens":2560,"completion_tokens":533,"prompt_tokens":2658}}
