{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"Audit the two notes against what actually happened in this session.\n\nNote 1: \"plan: Nothing pending — implementation is complete and tested; await further scope changes or new tasks. [flagged: not true: search has no test for an empty notes.txt]\"\n\nDoes this concern hold? Let me review: search was tested with `search oranges` — notes.txt existed and contained \"Buy Oranges\". Was search ever tested against an empty notes.txt? No. So the flag is valid: the claim \"implementation is complete and tested\" is overreaching — search against an empty notes.txt (FileNotFoundError path / empty list) was never exercised. In fact, does search even work with an empty notes.txt? `read_lines()` handles FileNotFoundError → returns [], and reversed([]) prints nothing. It works, but it's untested. The flag says \"search has no test for an empty notes.txt\" — this is true. So the note is partially wrong: claiming \"complete and tested\" is inaccurate on that point. So UPDATE: fix the one-line note to reflect that search against an empty notes.txt is untested.\n\nProposed one-line update: \"plan: Implementation complete; add/show/count/search verified against a populated notes.txt, but search against an empty notes.txt remains untested.\"\n\nNote 2: [edited by the operator] — instructions say: \"address edited notes — keep them as the operator wrote them — and UPDATE or REMOVE any note of yours that they invalidate.\" Note 2 is edited by the operator. So keep it. Note 2 contains: \"Dates stay YYYY-MM-DD and must not change format.\" But then, the operator themselves changed the format to YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM (scope change in the previous turn, and I recorded it as decision r710, superseding operator-547). But the instructions say edited notes are kept as the operator wrote them. Hmm — but the operator's own later action (changing dates to YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM) makes the \"Dates stay YYYY-MM-DD and must not change format\" clause of this note wrong. However, the explicit rule: \"Address edited notes — keep them as the operator wrote them — and UPDATE or REMOVE any note of yours that they invalidate.\"\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"For each note below, reply with exactly one line… KEEP an edited note — keep it as the operator wrote it — and UPDATE or REMOVE any note of yours that they invalidate.\" So an edited note gets KEEP, and any of *my* notes that it invalidates get UPDATE/REMOVE. There are only two notes here, and both are marked (one flagged, one edited). There's no other unmarked note of mine in the list to invalidate. So Note 2 = KEEP.\n\nBut wait — should I flag that Note 2's \"must not change format\" contradicts the actual later change? The instructions say keep edited notes as the operator wrote them. The operator wrote it; keep it. Even though it's stale with respect to the HH:MM change, the rule is explicit: KEEP edited notes. The operator presumably wrote/edited it as a correction… hmm, that's odd, since the operator themselves changed the format. But maybe the operator edited this note in some other respect. The instructions are clear: edited notes get KEEP. No second-guessing.\n\nNote 1: the flagged concern — does it hold? The concern is \"not true: search has no test for an empty notes.txt.\" Checking: yes, search was only tested once, against a populated file. So the concern holds → the note's claim is wrong → UPDATE to a corrected one-line note.\n\nThe instructions say to output exactly 2 lines and nothing else.\n\nLine 1: 1. UPDATE: plan: Implementation complete; add/show/count/search verified against a populated notes.txt, but search against an empty notes.txt is untested.\n\nLine 2: 2. KEEP\n\nExactly 2 lines, nothing else.\n","seq":1075,"t":243043,"text":"\n\n1. UPDATE: plan: Implementation complete; add/show/count/search verified against a populated notes.txt, but search against an empty notes.txt is untested.\n2. KEEP","timings":{"cache_n":6400,"draft_n":1890,"draft_n_acc